## Visual concept used

Reused the locked **Attractive "Architect's Elevation / Spec Sheet"** style, in its new Skin sub-archetype: an **outside-in WALL SECTION** where **layer thickness literally encodes the leverage proportion.** Four weather arrows strike the wall from outside with deliberately unequal magnitude — a giant alarm-red `PHOTOAGING · UV · ~80%` arrow dwarfing thin `INTRINSIC · TIME`, medium diet-side `GLYCATION · SUGAR`, and small `HORMONAL`. The protective assembly is drawn outside-in with dimensioned thicknesses: a thick load-bearing `PROTECT` slab (`~90%`, dimension-ticked) over a thin `CORE ACTIVES` membrane (`10%`), then a grade line into the ghosted-graphite **collagen scaffold** below grade carrying the finite-substrate caption, with small `PROCEDURES` repair brackets pinned in and an `ENHANCED` sliver (hazard tick). To the right sits the washed-out **reversed-assembly failure ghost** — PROTECT slab dashed/MISSING, a bright `LASER/SERUM ✔` interior patch installed, the unbroken red UV arrow pouring onto bare sagging scaffold, stamped `WRONG ORDER — paying to repair damage you are still causing.` Lower third = the slate-blue spec sheet (skin-age KPI dial `64→36 measured` + Visia/12-week rule; `TYPE BEFORE PROTOCOL` with Fitzpatrick III–V/PIH; the `FACE ≠ BODY` / `TOPICAL ≠ INTERNAL ≠ PROCEDURAL` boundary card). Footer = thesis lockup `PROTECT THE 90% BEFORE YOU POLISH THE 10%.` plus a four-chip decision strip (`PROTECT → TREAT → MULTIPLY → PUSH LAST`) whose chip sizes echo the layer thicknesses above.

Palette locked verbatim from the Attractive signature: bone paper `#F4F1EA`, graphite `#1E2024`, warm load-bearing terracotta `#C2703D`, cool instrument slate `#5E7585`, ghosted-grey scaffold, and signal red `#D9483B` rationed to the UV arrow, the reversed-ghost UV arrow, and the `WRONG ORDER` caption (plus tiny Rx/PIH hazard marks). Per §7: no split-face, no glowing-skin shot, no serum-bottle shelf as hero; UV visibly dwarfs the other forces; PROTECT visibly out-thicknesses everything above it.

